# width: 120
# height: 40
# daily: the Settings overlay has TWO key states and painted one footer
# for both. With the filter field focused (`/`), the line under the
# reader's hand still read `←→ adjust · ↑↓ move · … · Enter save` — but
# there `←→` move the text caret and Enter only hands the list back, so
# a daily driver who searched for a row and pressed `→` per the hint
# changed nothing, silently
# (hunt/findings-2026-09-21/daily-settings-filter-hint.md).
#
# The behaviour is deliberate and unit-tested; the footer is what was
# wrong. It is the field's own set while the field has the keys.
write notes.txt "alpha\n"
open notes.txt
command view.settings
expect screen contains "Launcher dock edge"
# The list's own footer, which is true of the list.
expect screen contains "adjust"

key /
type "dock edge"
expect screen contains "[bottom] / left / right"
# The field has the keys now, and the footer says what the field does.
expect screen contains "Enter to the list"
expect screen contains "Esc clears"
expect screen contains "caret"
expect screen lacks "adjust"
expect screen lacks "Enter save"

# And the keys it names are the keys that work: Enter hands the list
# back, keeping the query, and THEN `→` adjusts the row the search found.
key enter
expect screen contains "adjust"
expect screen lacks "Enter to the list"
key right
expect screen contains "bottom / [left] / right"

# `/` again puts the keys — and the footer — back in the field.
key /
expect screen contains "Enter to the list"
expect screen lacks "adjust"
key esc
expect screen contains "adjust"
key esc
